---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// Bump this to the date you actually re-verified the rows below (Step 2a).
const checked = '2026-08-31';

// Three states, not two. Rectangle's paid tier has custom sizes but no grid,
// and flattening that to "no" understates it — the same capability is why
// Divvy's and Moom's cells read yes.
const yes = { mark: '●', label: 'Yes' };
const no = { mark: '○', label: 'No' };
const pro = { mark: 'Pro', label: 'In the paid tier only' };

const rows = [
  { name: 'macOS built-in tiling', href: null,
    grid: no, regions: no, rebind: no, price: 'Free' },
  { name: 'Orthant', href: null, self: true,
    grid: yes, regions: yes, rebind: yes, price: 'Free' },
  { name: 'Rectangle', href: 'https://rectangleapp.com',
    grid: no, regions: pro, rebind: yes, price: 'Free · paid Pro tier' },
  { name: 'Divvy', href: 'https://mizage.com/divvy/',
    grid: yes, regions: yes, rebind: yes, price: '$13.99' },
  { name: 'Magnet', href: 'https://magnet.crowdcafe.com',
    grid: no, regions: no, rebind: yes, price: '$4.99' },
  { name: 'Moom', href: 'https://manytricks.com/moom/',
    grid: yes, regions: yes, rebind: yes, price: '$15' },
];
---
<Base
  title="How Orthant compares · Orthant"
  description="macOS already tiles windows. What a grid-based window manager adds on top, and how Orthant compares to Rectangle, Divvy, Magnet and Moom."
  path="/compare/"
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>How Orthant compares</h1>

    <h2>macOS already does halves and quarters. Why add anything?</h2>
    <p>
      Since Sequoia, macOS tiles windows in the box: halves, quarters, and drag-to-edge. For a
      lot of people that is genuinely enough, and if it is enough for you, you do not need
      Orthant.
    </p>
    <p>What it does not do:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No grid.</strong> You get the fractions Apple chose. There is no way to say
        "left two thirds" or "the middle column of five".</li>
      <li><strong>No custom regions.</strong> Nothing to name, save, or bind.</li>
      <li><strong>No rebinding.</strong> The shortcuts are fixed, and they hang off the Globe
        key.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Orthant adds those three things. Everything else about how macOS behaves is left alone, and
      the two coexist.
    </p>

    <h2>Against other window managers</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll-wrap">
      <div
        class="scroll-x"
        tabindex="0"
        role="region"
        aria-label="Comparison table. Five columns: App, Grid overlay, Custom regions, Rebindable, Price. Scrolls horizontally."
      >
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>App</th><th>Grid overlay</th><th>Custom regions</th><th>Rebindable</th><th>Price</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr class={r.self ? 'self' : undefined}>
              <td>{r.href ? <a href={r.href} rel="noopener">{r.name}</a> : r.name}</td>
              {[r.grid, r.regions, r.rebind].map((c) => (
                <td><span aria-hidden="true">{c.mark}</span><span class="visually-hidden">{c.label}</span></td>
              ))}
              <td>{r.price}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-fade" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>
    {/* A fade alone would be invisible to a screen-reader user and to
        anyone already scrolled to the end (spec-equivalent finding from the
        design gate) — this is real, non-hidden text, so it reaches both,
        and compare.ts hides it together with the fade the moment there is
        nothing left to scroll to. A plain line under the table rather than
        a badge floating over it, so it can never overlap the last column's
        own text. */}
    <p class="swipe-hint">Swipe to compare →</p>
    <p class="quiet">Prices and capabilities checked {checked}. Corrections welcome: <a
      href="https://github.com/orthant-app/orthant/issues">open an issue</a>. Magnet's own site
      lists no price; the figure above is from its Mac App Store listing.</p>
    <p class="cta"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/download">Download for macOS 13+</a></p>

    <h2>Which one should you use?</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Rectangle is excellent, free, and widely used.</strong> If you want reliable
      halves and quarters on keyboard shortcuts, use it. This is not a pitch to switch.
    </p>
    <p>
      Orthant is for the case Rectangle's free tier does not cover: when you want to{' '}
      <em>draw</em> where a window goes on a grid, and keep the result as a named shortcut.
      Rectangle <strong>Pro</strong> can define a custom size and position too. What it does
      not have is a grid to draw one on.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Divvy and Moom cover that ground too</strong>, as the table says. Both are
      long-standing, well-made apps, and if you already own one you do not need Orthant. The
      difference there is not capability. It is that Orthant is free and open source, and they
      are paid.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Table chrome comes from the shared `.prose table` rules in tokens.css —
     the docs tables and this one had drifted into two different looks. What
     stays here is only what is specific to this table. */
  table { min-width: 480px; }
  td:first-child, th:first-child { padding-left: 12px; }
  tr.self { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 6%, transparent); }
  .quiet { color: var(--label-secondary); font-size: 15px; }
  /* The capability glyphs are decorative; the real value is the adjacent text,
     which is the only thing a screen reader gets on the page most dependent on
     being read accurately. It uses the shared `.visually-hidden` from
     tokens.css — this file carried a second, identical copy under another
     name, so a fix to one could never reach the other. */

  /* Standalone links in a table cell are not "inline in a sentence", so the
     WCAG 2.2 target-size exemption does not cover them; at the row's own font
     size they were 18px tall. */
  td a { display: inline-block; padding-block: 4px; }

  /*
   * The scroll affordance the design gate asked for: a right-edge fade AND
   * an explicit "Swipe to compare" label, so a screen-reader user (who gets
   * nothing from a fade) and a sighted user who has already scrolled to the
   * end (who should not be told there is more) are both covered. Visibility
   * is driven by compare.ts's `data-can-scroll` attribute, not CSS alone —
   * a gradient cannot know on its own whether the scroller has reached its
   * last column.
   */
  .table-scroll-wrap {
    position: relative;
    /*
     * tokens.css exempts `.prose > .scroll-x` from the 68ch reading measure
     * because a comparison table should not wrap at reading width. Wrapping
     * `.scroll-x` one level deeper — required below — moves it out from
     * being a direct child of `.prose`, so that exemption has to be
     * restated for the new wrapper. Astro scopes this selector with a
     * per-component attribute, which gives it higher specificity than
     * `.prose > *`, so this wins without editing the shared file.
     */
    max-width: none;
  }
  /*
   * Positioned in this OUTER, non-scrolling wrapper, not as children of
   * `.scroll-x` itself: an absolutely positioned descendant of a scrolling
   * element is still part of that element's own scrollable content — its
   * offsets are resolved against the container's padding box, but it moves
   * WITH the content exactly like the table does, so a fade authored inside
   * `.scroll-x` would scroll out of view before the columns it is meant to
   * hint at. Being a sibling of `.scroll-x` keeps both pinned to the wrap's
   * edge regardless of scroll position.
   */
  .scroll-fade {
    position: absolute; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
    width: 40px;
    background: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, var(--surface));
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  /* A plain line under the table, not a badge floating over it — it can
     never overlap the last column's own text this way, at any width. */
  .swipe-hint {
    margin: 8px 0 0;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--label-secondary);
    text-align: right;
  }
  /* `visibility`, not `opacity`/`display`: it removes the fade from
     hit-testing and removes the hint from the accessibility tree once
     hidden, which is the other half of "hide it once scrolled to the end"
     — a label a screen reader can still reach after there is nothing left
     to scroll to is its own small version of the gate's original
     complaint. `visibility: hidden` still reserves the hint's line height,
     which is deliberate: showing or hiding it never shifts the paragraph
     below. */
  .scroll-fade, .swipe-hint { visibility: hidden; }
  .table-scroll-wrap[data-can-scroll] .scroll-fade,
  /* Adjacent-sibling, not a descendant selector: .swipe-hint is a sibling
     of .table-scroll-wrap, not inside it (kept out so it can never overlap
     the table it describes). */
  .table-scroll-wrap[data-can-scroll] + .swipe-hint { visibility: visible; }

  .cta { margin-top: 28px; }
</style>

<script>
  import { attachScrollHint } from '../lib/compare';
  const wrap = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.table-scroll-wrap');
  if (wrap) attachScrollHint(wrap);
</script>
